# Pagoda Travel — engineering standards

Full guide: `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root. This file is the enforced subset.

Next.js 16 App Router · React 19 · TypeScript · Tailwind 4 · Supabase.
Five portals: `app/{admin,agent,agency,guide}` + operator flows. Business logic lives in flat
`lib/` modules (~120 of them — grep before adding one). Route handlers stay thin.

## 1. Silent failures are bugs

**A fallback that returns a different result than the caller asked for is a bug, not
resilience.** If the system cannot do what was requested, return an error the user can read.

Every production incident in this repo has been this shape: a chat "new thread" button that
returned the existing thread with `ok: true`; a `job_hiring_history` insert that failed
`23502`, was logged, and still reported the booking as successful; an email marked
`emailQueued: true` from a promise nobody awaited.

- `void somePromise()` only for genuinely optional side effects — and the caller must **never
  report success on its behalf**. If the user is told it happened, `await` it.
- A destructured Supabase `error` that is never read is a review failure.
- Never widen a `catch` to hide a failure you have not diagnosed.

## 2. Auth

```ts
const session = await requireSessionActor();        // lib/itinerary-access
if (!session.ok) return session.response;
const { userId, role, isAdmin } = session.actor;
```

Never read `userId` / `role` cookies directly in a new route. **Impersonation** (admin
overall-access) is read via `readImpersonation()` in `lib/admin-impersonation.ts` — during
impersonation the `role` cookie says `agent`/`guide` and only `impersonator_id` reveals the
real actor. Any code deciding *who did this* or *who to notify* must check it.

Access guards: `assertItineraryAccess`, `assertJobItineraryAccess`, `assertUserCanAccessChat`.

## 3. Responses

Use `lib/api-response.ts` (`ok`, `fail`, `unauthorized`, `forbidden`, `notFound`,
`migrationRequired`). Shape is always `{ ok: true, ... }` or `{ ok: false, error }`. Never
leak raw DB text to an end user — log it, return something a travel advisor can act on. When
the schema is behind the code, say so and name the migration.

Most routes still hand-roll `NextResponse.json`. Migrate what you touch; do not sweep working
routes you have no other reason to open.

## 4. Validation

Parse at the edge with `lib/validate.ts`. Money → `parseMoney`; percentages →
`parseMarkupPct`; activity types → `canonicalizeActivityTypeLabel`. No `as any` on a request
body.

## 5. Logging

`lib/ops-log.ts` only — no bare `console.log` in new code. Tags `PagodaBooking` / `PagodaChat`
/ `PagodaMail` / `PagodaTransfer` / `PagodaAuth`; step names are `noun.verb`
(`email.sent`, `message.stored`); structured data object, never string interpolation — these
are grepped in Vercel during incidents. Never log full emails where an id will do, phone
numbers, tokens, or cookies.

## 6. Pricing

One module: `lib/pagoda-pricing.ts` (+ `lib/pagoda-sales-calculator.ts`).

```
purchase JPY → × (1+FX%) → × (1+marketplace%) = sales JPY
  → ÷ USD/JPY rate = sales USD
  → × agent% = advisor commission (share of sales, not a second markup)
```

Commissions live per-guide in `guide_commission_settings`, set at `/admin/commission-settings`,
and are read **live** on every computation — Tour Library, itinerary lines, confirmation emails
and PDF must move together. Never hardcode a markup percentage.

## 7. Migrations

Hand-run on Supabase. Idempotent (`IF NOT EXISTS`), dated `YYYYMMDD_description.sql`, header
comment explaining **why** and what failure it repairs, `COMMENT ON COLUMN` for anything
non-obvious. Code depending on a new column must degrade with a clear error — production may
not have run it yet.

## 8. Verification

No test framework. Behaviour that cannot be unit-tested gets a guard script in `scripts/`
wired into `package.json` (`npm run test:*`). Add one every time you fix a silent failure.
Before deploy: `npx tsc --noEmit` && `npm run lint` && all guards.

## 9. Style

2-space indent; match the surrounding file for quotes/semicolons (`lib/` is double quotes +
semicolons, some older `app/api/` routes are single quotes without). Named exports. `type` over
`interface` for data shapes. Comments explain **why**. Keep route handlers under ~200 lines.

## 10. Known debt — do not widen

`app/agent/edit-itinerary/page.tsx` and `app/agency/edit-itinerary/page.tsx` are near-identical
and drift constantly. When you change one, check the other.
